y": 0.6}, "rating_margin": 12.5, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.11, "baseline_margin": 12.3, "analyst_adjustment": -0.8, "key_factors": ["Favorable Democratic environment in a D+3 open seat", "Matt Little's roughly two-to-one fundraising and outside-spending edge", "Prediction markets price a Democratic win at 94-95%", "Thin, sponsor-skewed polling leaves residual uncertainty"], "rationale": "A slightly Democratic district, a favorable national environment, and Little's large money advantage point to a comfortable Democratic margin; the lone recent poll shows a 12-point race and markets price Democratic odds in the mid-90s. The open seat and sparse independent polling keep this just short of the safest category.", "watch": "Late Republican outside spending or new polling showing a single-digit race in the south suburbs would tighten the outlook.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Angie Craig (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Minnesota's 2nd District is an open seat after Angie Craig left to run for Senate, and Democrats enter the general election with clear advantages. The district leans slightly Democratic, the national environment favors the party, and nominee Matt Little has out-raised Republican Eric Pratt by roughly two to one while drawing nearly all of the outside spending. A Democratic-sponsored September poll showed Little ahead 52-40, while a Republican-sponsored June poll had a narrower 45-40 contest.\n\nPrediction markets put Democratic win odds around 94-95 percent, and race raters split between Likely and Safe Democratic. The main questions are whether Little, a more progressive nominee than Craig, holds together the shifting south-suburban coalition, and whether Pratt can close the money gap in the final weeks. The race has been quiet, with limited independent polling to track late movement.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9565, "margin": 11.37}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9477, "margin": 11.0}, {"date